---
import ContentLayout from '@/layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Accordion from '@/components/ui/Accordion.astro';
import { imageUrl } from '@lib/images';
import { faqJsonLd } from '@lib/jsonld';
const toc = [
  { id: 'hand-vs-machine', label: 'Hand-knotted vs machine-made' },
  { id: 'knots', label: 'Knot types' },
  { id: 'kpsi', label: 'What KPSI means' },
  { id: 'silk', label: 'Silk vs art silk' },
  { id: 'dyes', label: 'Natural vs synthetic dyes' },
  { id: 'age', label: 'New, vintage, antique' },
  { id: 'certificate', label: 'What the certificate means' },
  { id: 'guarantee', label: 'The guarantee' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
];
const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Can I verify a rug myself after it arrives?',
    a: 'Yes, and I hope you do. Flip it and compare the back to the video. Pull one loose fiber from the fringe and burn it: wool smells like burning hair and leaves a brittle black bead. Measure it. Count knots in one inch, across and down.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you use the word “handmade” loosely?',
    a: 'No. Everything here is hand-knotted (pile) or hand-woven (kilim) on a loom by a person. “Hand-tufted” rugs, which are shot through a canvas with a tufting gun and glued, are not sold here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do you list dyes as “unknown” sometimes?',
    a: 'Because sometimes I can’t tell. Well-made synthetic dyes from the 1980s onward can look natural. If a dealer’s claim can’t be backed up by what I see, I write “unknown” rather than guess.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Authenticity guide · spot a real hand-knotted rug"
  description="How to tell hand-knotted from machine-made, silk from art silk, what KPSI means, natural vs synthetic dyes, and what a certificate should actually mean."
  h1="How to tell a real rug from a script"
  intro="Everything I check before I buy a rug, written down so you can check it too. [EDIT: expand to 1,500–2,500 words with real photos]"
  updated="2026-09-28"
  toc={toc}
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd(faq)]}
>
  <section id="hand-vs-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Hand-knotted vs machine-made</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Flip it.</strong> That is the whole test. On a hand-knotted rug, the back shows the
      design clearly, in slightly softer colors, and each knot is a small, slightly irregular
      square. Rows wobble a little. The fringe is the ends of the warp threads that run through the
      whole rug, not a strip sewn on afterward.
    </p>
    <p>
      A machine-made rug shows a mechanically perfect grid on the back, often with a stiff latex or
      canvas backing, and the fringe is stitched on. Tufted rugs have a glued canvas back that hides
      everything, which is why they hide everything.
    </p>
    <img
      src={imageUrl('sample-guide-knots', 'gallery')}
      alt="Comparison of the back of a hand-knotted rug showing irregular knots beside a machine-made rug showing a uniform grid [EDIT: real comparison photo]"
      width="1600"
      height="900"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="my-6 rounded-card"
    />
    <p>
      Two more tells: look at the edges. Hand-knotted rugs are rarely perfect rectangles. And press
      your thumb into the pile; a good wool pile springs back.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="knots" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Knot types</h2>
    <p>
      Turkish rugs use the <strong>symmetrical (Ghiordes) knot</strong>: the yarn wraps around two
      warps and the two ends come up between them. Persian rugs mostly use the{' '}
      <strong>asymmetrical (Senneh) knot</strong>, wrapped around one warp and looped under the
      other. On the back, symmetrical knots look like two little bumps side by side, asymmetrical
      knots like one. Neither is better; it’s a regional signature and a good clue to where a rug is
      from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kpsi" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>What KPSI means</h2>
    <p>
      Knots per square inch. Count the knots across one inch on the back, then down one inch, and
      multiply. Village rugs run 30–60. Workshop Oushaks 50–100. Fine city rugs 150 and up. Higher
      means finer detail and more months on the loom, not a longer life. A 40-KPSI village rug with
      good wool will outlast a 200-KPSI rug with poor wool.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>KPSI</th>
          <th>Typical of</th>
          <th>What it means for you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>30–60</td>
          <td>Village, tribal, most vintage Oushaks</td>
          <td>Bold drawing, thick pile, very durable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>60–120</td>
          <td>Workshop rugs, new Oushaks</td>
          <td>Cleaner curves, mid pile</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>120+</td>
          <td>City workshops, Hereke, fine antiques</td>
          <td>Detailed, low pile, priced by the hour</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="silk" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Silk vs art silk</h2>
    <p>
      The most common tourist-shop trick is selling <strong>viscose</strong> (“art silk,” “bamboo
      silk,” “banana silk”) as silk. Viscose is a processed plant cellulose; it looks shiny and
      wears badly. The burn test settles it: pull one loose fiber from the fringe and burn it.{' '}
      <strong>Real silk</strong> smells like burning hair and leaves a crushable black ash.{' '}
      <strong>Viscose</strong> smells like burning paper and leaves a soft gray ash. Wool also
      smells like hair, so combine the burn with a look: silk has a cool, deep luster; viscose a
      flat, plasticky shine.
    </p>
    <p>
      On this site the word “silk” only appears in a listing when the fiber content says so, and I
      have burned a fiber to check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="dyes" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Natural vs synthetic dyes</h2>
    <p>
      Natural dyes (madder root for reds, indigo for blues, weld and onion skin for yellows, walnut
      for browns) sit unevenly in the wool. That unevenness is called <strong>abrash</strong>: soft
      horizontal bands where the dye lot changed. Natural dyes also fade gently and evenly.
      Synthetic dyes are flatter, more saturated, and, in cheap versions, can bleed when wet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Neither is wrong. A good modern rug with colorfast synthetic dyes is a good rug. What matters
      is that the seller tells you. I list every rug as natural, synthetic, mixed, or unknown.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="age" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>New, vintage, antique</h2>
    <p>
      I use three plain buckets. <strong>New</strong>: made in the last five years.{' '}
      <strong>Vintage</strong>: roughly 20–50 years old, often with softened colors and low, even
      pile. <strong>Antique</strong>: 100 years or more, priced as collector pieces and photographed
      with every worn area. Dealers’ age claims get downgraded when I can’t back them up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="certificate" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>What the certificate means</h2>
    <p>
      Every tourist shop hands out a “certificate of authenticity.” Most are a printed word. Mine is
      a record of what I checked: rug ID, photo, size in feet and centimeters, pile and foundation
      fibers, dye type, knot count, region, age bucket, condition notes, and my signature. The ID on
      the certificate matches the page you bought from, and that page never disappears. If the
      certificate and the rug ever disagree, the guarantee below applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="guarantee" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>The guarantee</h2>
    <blockquote>
      If an independent appraiser finds that a rug isn’t what we described, we’ll refund you in
      full, including return shipping.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That’s the whole policy. It’s on every product page and in the{' '}
      <a href="/terms">terms of sale</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Authenticity FAQ</h2>
    <Accordion items={faq.map((f) => ({ title: f.q, content: `<p>${f.a}</p>` }))} name="auth-faq" />
  </section>
  <p class="mt-10">
    Ready to see the real thing? <a href="/rugs">Browse the collection</a> or read about{' '}
    <a href="/collections/oushak">Oushak rugs</a>.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
